From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: sunxi-ng: a733: the NPU's PLL, module clock, bus gates and resets

The NPU is the last compute block on this SoC with nothing in our CCU. No NPU
clock and no NPU reset is modelled, so PLL_NPU, NPU_CLK_REG and NPU_BGR_REG
keep whatever the boot chain left in them and no consumer can claim them. This
patch adds six clocks and three resets and does nothing else -- nothing in the
tree references any of them until the device tree in `arm64-dts-a733-add-the-npu` does.

WHERE THE VALUES COME FROM
--------------------------
Register geometry from the CCU field tables of the A733 user manual (extracted
from A733 UM V0.92, 4.1.6), every field re-read in the UM text. CCU base 0x02002000, so absolute
= base + offset. Absolute addresses are given beside every offset throughout,
because an offset read against the wrong base returns a plausible number
rather than an error -- which has already cost this tree a session.

  PLL_NPU_CTRL_REG   0x02A0 / abs 0x020022A0  reset 0x4010_4500
    31     PLL_EN            0 Disable 1 Enable, reset 0
    30     PLL_LDO_EN        reset 0x1
    29     LOCK_ENABLE       reset 0x0
    28     LOCK (R)          valid only while LOCK_ENABLE (bit 29) is 1
    27     PLL_OUTPUT_GATE   reset 0x0
    26:23  reserved
    22:20  PLL_OUTPUT_DIV    P0 = field + 1, field 0..7, reset 0x1
    15:8   PLL_N             N = field + 1, field 0..254, reset 0x45,
                             field value must be at least 11
    1      PLL_INPUT_DIV     M1 = field + 1, field 0..1, reset 0x0
    the register's own formula: "The NPUPLL = 24MHz*N/M1/P0"
  NPU_CLK_REG        0x0B00 / abs 0x02002B00  reset 0x0000_0000
    31     NPU_CLK_GATING    0 = off, 1 = on
    26:24  CLK_SRC_SEL       000 NPUPLL     001 PERI0_800M  010 PERI0_600M
                             011 PERI0_480M 100 VE0PLL      101 VE1PLL
                             110 DEPLL3X
    4:0    FACTOR_M          M = field + 1, field 0..31
    NPU_CLK = clock source / M.
  NPU_BGR_REG        0x0B04 / abs 0x02002B04  reset 0x0008_0000
    19 NPU_SRAM_RST (reset 0x1)  18 NPU_AHB_RST (0x0)
    17 NPU_AXI_RST  (reset 0x0)  16 NPU_CORE_RST (0x0)
       all four: 0 = Assert, 1 = De-assert
    0  NPU_GATING   0 = Mask, 1 = Pass, reset 0x0
  AHB_MAT_CLK_GATING_REG   0x05C0 / abs 0x020025C0  reset 0x1101_03FF
    6  NPU_AHB_GATE_SW_CFG   reset 0x1
  MBUS_MAT_CLK_GATING_REG  0x05E0 / abs 0x020025E0  reset 0xF105_5803
    18 NPU_MBUS_GATE_SW_CFG  reset 0x1
  Table 4-2, PLL_NPU row: Fvco 1.26G~2.52G, pre-divider 1/2, typical VCO
    1.8G
    ⚠ P0 1..8 does not appear in the PLL_NPU row of the Table 4-2 text extract --
    the extract is column-major and the "P0: 1~8" tokens for this row land
    among PLL_DE's. The authority for P0 used here is the PLL_NPU_CTRL_REG
    field table in the A733 user manual.
  Table 4-3 typical configuration, the row between DDR_PLL and PLL_DE:
    DCXO 24, N 70, VCO 1680, OUT 840

SILICON -- THREE STATES, TWO DRIVER STACKS, AND THEY DECIDE THE DESIGN
----------------------------------------------------------------------
The 6 GiB board's readings were not taken by the author; they come from a
separate NPU investigation on 2026-08-25. The vendor-kernel board's readings are this
patch's own, READ-ONLY through /sys/class/sunxi_dump/dump with no register
written.

  register                the 6 GiB board idle    the 6 GiB board resumed   vendor-kernel board, idle
                          (etnaviv)    (etnaviv)      (vendor 6.6 vipcore)
  PLL_NPU_CTRL 0x020022A0 0x01104500   0xf9104500     0x01105300
  NPU_CLK      0x02002B00 0x00000000   0x80000000     0x00000000
  NPU_BGR      0x02002B04 0x000f0000   0x000f0001     0x00080000
  AHB_MAT      0x020025C0 --           --             0x110103b8
  MBUS_MAT     0x020025E0 --           --             0xf0010803
  NPUPLL_GATE  0x02003920 0x00010000   --             0x00010000
  CM_NPU_CFG   0x02003B1C 0x00020000   --             0x00010000

What each column settles:

  - PLL_NPU IS NOT A FIXED RATE. N = 0x45 + 1 = 70 on the 6 GiB board (VCO 1680, out
    840 MHz) and N = 0x53 + 1 = 84 on a board on the vendor kernel (VCO 2016, out 1008 MHz),
    P0 = 2 in both. A clk_fixed_rate would be wrong at one of those two rates
    whichever number it hardcoded, which is the same argument `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset` made for
    PLL_DE. So it is a ccu_nm that reads the register.
  - THE RESET DEFAULT IS READABLE AS A FREQUENCY, and it is 840 MHz, not the
    1.8 GHz that Table 4-2's "typical VCO" column invites. 0x4010_4500 is
    N = 70, M1 = 1, P0 = 2 -> 24 * 70 / 1 / 2, which is exactly Table 4-3's own
    row. PLL_EN resets to 0, so that is what the reset FIELDS encode, not an
    output present at reset. (⚠ Table 4-2 and Table 4-3 disagree on five rows,
    PLL_NPU among them; the UM never says which wins. It changes nothing here:
    both numbers are inside the Fvco window and this driver programs neither.)
  - PLL_LDO_EN CANNOT BE TRUSTED TO ITS RESET DEFAULT. The UM gives bit 30 a
    default of 0x1; both idle readings have it CLEAR (0x01104500 / 0x01105300).
    A PLL whose LDO is off cannot lock however long the framework polls, so
    probe() forces LDO_EN and LOCK_ENABLE, exactly as it already does for the
    video PLLs and PLL_DE. The resumed reading 0xf9104500 is the proof that
    with them set the PLL locks at the untouched N: PLL_EN, LDO_EN,
    LOCK_ENABLE, LOCK and OUTPUT_GATE all 1.
  - THE THREE RESETS ARE NOT DECORATION. A board on the vendor kernel reads NPU_BGR =
    0x00080000, the bare UM reset value: SRAM de-asserted, and AHB, AXI and
    CORE all still ASSERTED. (The 6 GiB board is found the other way, 0x000f0000.) A board
    is genuinely found with them held, so a consumer has to release them.
  - THE TWO BUS-MATRIX GATES MOVE. Both reset ENABLED, so the cheap conclusion
    is that nothing ever touches them -- the conclusion `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset` and `arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-node-and-de-to-tcon-edge` drew for the
    display engine's matrix gates. It is false here: with the NPU
    runtime-suspended a board on the vendor kernel reads AHB_MAT bit 6 CLEAR (0x110103b8) and
    MBUS_MAT bit 18 CLEAR (0xf0010803), while CM_NPU_STATUS reads 01 = Power
    OFF. They are per-use software gates that a running stack turns off with
    the block, which is why they are modelled as clocks and handed to the
    consumer rather than written down in a comment. The same file already does
    this for the same two registers' SERDES bits (bus-serdes 0x05C0 BIT(8),
    mbus-serdes 0x05E0 BIT(28), from `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-serdes-combophy-clock`) -- and the reason is on the record:
    USB3 linked at 10 Gbps and still would not come up on a normal boot,
    because dwc3 reached registers behind bus-matrix gates nothing declared.
  - THE MUX HAS NEVER BEEN ANYTHING BUT 000 = NPUPLL, across two unrelated
    driver stacks and three states.

THE PLL IS SHARED, AND IT WAS SWEPT BEFORE ITS ENABLE WAS MODELLED
------------------------------------------------------------------
This is the question that has to be answered before PLL_EN may be a clock's
own enable, because clk_disable_unused() can then clear it. UM figure 4-2
"PLL Distribution" lists PLL_NPU as feeding **MBUS, VE, NPU and DRAM0**
(A733 user manual) -- and two of those would take the SoC down with them. It
is the same question `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset` had to answer for PLL_DE, and it gets the same kind
of answer: an exhaustive register sweep, not an argument.

Exactly FIVE CCU registers name NPUPLL in a source mux at all. That is not a
guess either: it is every register in the A733 user manual whose
field enum contains the string, and it comes out as the figure's list with VE
counted twice. All five read on a board on the vendor kernel while it was up and running,
2026-08-25:

  register           abs           value        mux -> source
  MBUS_CLK_REG       0x02002588    0x81000000   001 PERI0_600M
  VE_ENC0_CLK_REG    0x02002a80    0x01000000   001 VE1PLL     (gate off)
  VE_DEC_CLK_REG     0x02002a88    0x01000000   001 VE0PLL     (gate off)
  DRAM0_CLK_REG      0x02002c00    0x80000003   000 DDRPLL
  NPU_CLK_REG        0x02002b00    0x00000000   000 NPUPLL     (gate off)

Not one of the four other consumers is on NPUPLL. And the decisive reading
needs no interpretation at all: **PLL_EN is already 0 on that running board**
(PLL_NPU_CTRL = 0x01105300), so nothing on it can be running off this PLL.

⚠ THE LIMIT OF THAT SWEEP, NAMED. All three PLL_NPU readings in this patch
come from somebody else's operating system -- vendor 6.6 on the vendor-kernel board,
mainline etnaviv on the 6 GiB board. There is NO cold read of PLL_NPU from OUR OWN boot
chain, which is what `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset` had for PLL_DE ("read at the U-Boot prompt on a cold
power-cycle -- nothing had run"). The sweep says no other consumer is muxed
onto NPUPLL under two foreign stacks; it does not, strictly, say our chain
leaves the muxes the same way. Cheap to close and worth closing at the next
U-Boot prompt: read 0x020022A0, 0x02002588, 0x02002c00 cold.

WHAT THIS CHANGES ON A KERNEL WITH NO NPU DRIVER BOUND
------------------------------------------------------
One thing, and it is named here so it is not discovered later:
clk_disable_unused() now clears AHB_MAT bit 6 and MBUS_MAT bit 18, 1 -> 0,
because they are clocks and nothing claims them. That is the observable this
patch owns. It is safe on two grounds -- both fields are NPU-only by their own
names (NPU_AHB_GATE_SW_CFG / NPU_MBUS_GATE_SW_CFG), and it is the state a
vendor stack already leaves this silicon in whenever the NPU is idle.

Everything else this patch can reach is already 0 on an idle board, PLL_EN and
the PLL output gate included, so clk_disable_unused() writes zeros over zeros
there -- and the sweep above is why that is a fact about the SoC and not just
about one register. No CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED, as everywhere else in this file.

WHAT THE A523 SAYS, AND WHY NONE OF IT WAS TAKEN
------------------------------------------------
⚠ The first draft of this patch asserted "the A523 has no NPU at all". That is
FALSE, it is deleted rather than struck through, and the way it was caught is
worth one line: a grep for the clock name "npu" across drivers/clk/, run to
check for a global-name collision, hit ccu-sun55i-a523.c. Had the assertion
stood, the section that is supposed to guard against A523 provenance would
itself have been the reason nobody looked.

ccu-sun55i-a523.c DOES model an NPU, and it is the dangerous kind of source:
the shape is exactly what is wanted here. Its OFFSETS, its enable bit, its tap
topology and its parent set are all wrong for this part.

⚠ It is NOT true that "every number in it is wrong", which is what an earlier
draft of this section claimed. Four of them are identical and correct on both
parts: .lock = BIT(28), the N field geometry (shift 8, width 8, offset 1), .m
= _SUNXI_CCU_DIV(1, 1), and the module clock's M [4:0] / mux [26:24] widths.
An overclaim in the section whose job is to catch A523 numbers is worse than
no section at all, so the list below is the one that survives checking.

The offsets are the expensive half: both are undocumented HOLES on the A733 --
neither 0x080 nor 0x6E0 is among the CCU registers in
the A733 user manual's register list, whose nearest neighbours are PLL_DDR_BIAS_REG 0x030 /
PLL_PERI0_CTRL_REG 0x0A0 and MBUS_GATE_EN_REG 0x5E4 / DMA0_BGR_REG 0x704. A
carried-over model would write into nothing, leave the block dark, and every
write would look like it had succeeded. That is the same failure `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-edp-bus-gate-and-reset` avoided
on the eDP gate, with the same shape.

  A523 (ccu-sun55i-a523.c)            A733 (UM 4.1.6, and this patch)
  PLL_NPU reg 0x080          :319     PLL_NPU_CTRL_REG 0x02A0
  .enable = BIT(27)          :321     PLL_EN is BIT(31). On the A733 bit 27 is
                                      PLL_OUTPUT_GATE, so the inherited model
                                      would toggle the output gate, never
                                      enable the PLL, and STILL report a rate,
                                      because ccu_nm_recalc_rate() reads the
                                      factor fields whether or not PLL_EN is
                                      set. Green build, plausible clk_summary,
                                      dead silicon.
  _SUNXI_CCU_MULT_MIN(8,8,11) :323    Same arithmetic, different BOUNDS.
                                      ⚠ An earlier draft called this an offset
                                      defect -- "N is the raw field, no +1".
                                      False: _SUNXI_CCU_MULT_MIN expands with
                                      offset 1 (ccu_mult.h:26), so both parts
                                      compute N = field + 1. What actually
                                      differs: its min 11 admits field 10, one
                                      step under the UM's minimum of 11
                                      for the PLL_N field (a bound on
                                      the FIELD, hence N >= 12 here); and its
                                      max 0 becomes 1 << width = 256 in
                                      ccu_nm.c, one above the UM's field range
                                      0..254, hence N <= 255 here.
  TWO fixed-factor taps, /2 and /4    ONE tap, and PROGRAMMABLE:
    (:332, :335), hung off the        PLL_OUTPUT_DIV [22:20], P0 = field + 1,
    ccu_nm itself, which is the "4x"  range 1..8
  CLK_SET_RATE_GATE          :329     CLK_SET_RATE_UNGATE -- the lock poll in
                                      ccu_nm_set_rate() has to run on a PLL
                                      that is on
  npu module clock at 0x6e0  :496     NPU_CLK_REG 0x0B00
  4 parents: PERI0_480M, 600M,        7 parents: NPUPLL, PERI0_800M, 600M,
    800M, PLL_NPU_2X   :490-495       480M, VE0PLL, VE1PLL, DEPLL3X. Different
                                      set AND different order, so even the
                                      three shared parents sit at different
                                      indices -- an inherited parent array
                                      would mis-name the source of a running
                                      clock rather than fail.
  no NPU reset in its reset header    three: NPU_BGR bits 16 / 17 / 18
  no NPU bus gate, no matrix gates    NPU_BGR bit 0, AHB_MAT bit 6,
                                      MBUS_MAT bit 18

The only thing that crosses over is the module clock's divider and mux WIDTHS
(M [4:0], mux [26:24]), and they were re-read in the A733
user manual rather than taken: a width that happens to agree is
still not evidence. And two of our seven parents, VE0PLL and VE1PLL, are
clocks this tree cannot name at all -- something no A523 file would ever have
told anyone.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT MODELLED, each for a stated reason
-----------------------------------------------------------
  NPU_BGR bit 19, NPU_SRAM_RST -- the fourth reset. It is the only one of the
      four that resets DE-asserted, and no consumer in this tree asks for it;
      an id nothing references is dead code, the same call `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock` made for
      PLL_VIDEO's 3X tap. Named in the reset header so it is not mistaken for
      a gap.
  NPUPLL_GATE_EN_REG 0x1920 / abs 0x02003920 and its read-only twin
      NPUPLL_GATE_STAT_REG 0x19A0 -- a second gate stage in series with the
      PLL's own PLL_OUTPUT_GATE. bit16 NPUPLL_GATE_SW_CFG (reset 1 = Enable),
      bit0 NPUPLL_AUTO_GATE_EN (reset 1 = No-Auto), register reset
      0x0001_0001.
      ⚠ AN EARLIER DRAFT OF THIS ROW WAS WRONG IN BOTH HALVES and is corrected
      rather than softened. It said the boards' 0x0001_0000 was "exactly the UM
      reset value" and that "no stack we have measured writes it". The reset
      value is 0x0001_0001, so 0x0001_0000 means bit0 has been CLEARED --
      switched from the reset "No-Auto" to "Auto" -- and something therefore
      does write it. (0x0001_0000 is the reset value of the read-only STATUS
      twin at 0x19A0, which is where the wrong number came from.)
      The correct reason to leave it unmodelled is the narrower one this file
      already gives for DEPLL_GATE_EN_REG (0x1928) in the PLL_DE block: the SW GATE
      needs no write. The vendor makes the same move on DEPLL_GATE_EN 0x1928
      (0x00030000 against a 0x00030003 reset) and on VIDEOPLL_GATE_EN abs
      0x02003910 (0x00770000) -- it is auto-gating, a power feature this tree
      does not implement. Modelling the SW gate as a clock would only hand
      clk_disable_unused() a second way to stop the PLL output.
      (⚠ These two offsets were first transcribed WRONG, until 2026-08-25,
      as 0x0F14/0x0F1C, which are SPI2_BGR_REG and SPIF_BGR_REG. The
      corrected values are used here.)
  CM_NPU_CFG_REG 0x1B1C / abs 0x02003B1C. Its bit 0 CM_NPU_MODULE_MODE looks
      like a module gate and is not the effective one: it reads 0 with the
      domain OFF and 0 with the domain ON, while bits [17:16] CM_NPU_STATUS
      move 01 <-> 10 (measured on the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-25;
      a board on the vendor kernel reads 01 = Power OFF with its NPU suspended).
      The same finding already holds for CM_DESYS on the DE, CM_VO1 on the
      TCON path and CM_USB2 on USB. The power island is reached through the
      PCK-600 genpd provider, which `arm64-dts-a733-add-the-npu` wires with power-domains.

⚠ AND ALL THREE RESETS ARE IN sun60iw2_ccu_resets[], not only in the header.
A reset defined in a binding header and referenced from a device tree but
missing from that array makes reset_control_deassert() a SILENT no-op that
returns success -- nothing in the build checks it, and it is exactly what happened to
RST_BUS_GMAC0 / RST_BUS_GMAC0_AXI, which cost a board session and a false
hardware proof. Verified by
inspecting the compiled object, not by reading the diff -- see below.

VERIFIED / NOT VERIFIED
-----------------------
  applies      yes -- full series to a freshly unpacked 6.18.36, fuzz 0.
  builds       yes -- compile check.
  arrays       yes, read out of the built object rather than the diff -- the
               method `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset`'s header used. In ccu-sun60iw2.o:
                 sun60iw2_ccu_clks    0x218 = 536 B = 67 entries (was 61, +6)
                 sun60iw2_hw_clks     0x288 = 648 B = 8 + 80 * 8, so
                                      .num == CLK_MBUS_NPU + 1 == 80 (was 74)
                 sun60iw2_ccu_resets  0x0f8 = 248 B = 31 entries (was 28, +3)
               The reset array is the one that matters: RST_BUS_NPU_AHB = 30
               is the last entry, so all three resets really are IN the map
               and not only in the header.
  HW-effect    NOT OBSERVED. No board was touched by this patch and none is
               owed by it: nothing in this tree consumes an NPU clock yet.
               The register reads quoted above are INPUT evidence, not proof
               of this patch.

PENDING OBSERVABLE, so the next person knows what would falsify it: on a
kernel carrying this patch and `arm64-dts-a733-add-the-npu`, with an NPU driver bound and resumed,
abs 0x020022A0 shows PLL_EN|LDO_EN|LOCK_ENABLE|LOCK|OUTPUT_GATE set,
abs 0x02002B00 bit 31 = 1 with CLK_SRC_SEL = 000, abs 0x02002B04 = 0x000f0001,
and abs 0x020025C0 bit 6 / abs 0x020025E0 bit 18 both back to 1.

