+/*
+ * The single output tap: PLL_OUTPUT_DIV [22:20], linear, P0 = field + 1 over
+ * a field range of 0..7, gated by PLL_OUTPUT_GATE
+ * bit 27. Unlike PLL_DE this PLL has exactly one output, so there is one tap
+ * and not two -- bits 26:23 are reserved here, not a
+ * second divider.
+ *
+ * CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT so one clk_set_rate() on the module clock configures
+ * divider, tap and PLL, the idiom `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock` and `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset` already use on this SoC.
+ * CLK_SET_RATE_UNGATE on the PLL above is what makes the lock poll mean
+ * anything: clk_change_rate() enables the clock before calling ->set_rate, so
+ * ccu_helper_wait_for_lock() inside ccu_nm_set_rate() runs on a PLL that is
+ * actually on, and the output gate is this separate clock, opened by the
+ * consumer afterwards.
+ *
+ * ⚠ That is NOT the UM's Method 2, and an earlier draft of this comment said
+ * it was. Method 2 is: LDO_EN, then write N/M1/M0/P, then
+ * PLL_EN, then LOCK_ENABLE, then wait LOCK, then wait 20 us, then
+ * PLL_OUTPUT_GATE. The framework does steps 2 and 3 the other way round --
+ * PLL_EN goes on first and the factors are written into a running PLL -- and
+ * ccu_helper_wait_for_lock() has no post-lock delay, so step 6 is absent
+ * entirely. This matches what every other PLL in this file does and no
+ * misbehaviour has been observed, but it is a deviation, not compliance.
+ *
+ * ⚠ CONSTRAINT ON THE NPU DRIVER, because neither the device tree nor this
+ * file can express it: set the rate BEFORE enabling the clock. ccu_nm_ops
+ * waits for lock only inside ccu_nm_set_rate(), so a driver that enables
+ * first opens the output gate on a PLL that may not have locked.
